WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.250
From three objects, A, B and C, choose any group,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.890
including the empty group with nothing in it.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.120
Explain why exactly half of the groups have an even

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.070
number of members.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.220
Each object is either in the group or out of it.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.820
Two choices, three times over, gives eight groups.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.730
Pair each group with the group you get by changing A:

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.950
put A in if it is out, and take A out if it is in.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.390
The empty group pairs with the group containing only A.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.310
The group B, C pairs with the group A, B, C.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.610
Changing A changes the size of the group by exactly one.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.900
So each pair has one group of even size and one

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:45.650
of odd size.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.750
Four pairs give four even groups and four odd groups.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.730
Check by listing.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.400
The even groups are the empty group, A with B, A with C,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.430
and B with C.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.730
That is four, exactly half of eight.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.550
Toggling one member pairs every even group with

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.320
an odd one.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.510
Rebuild this argument, then say how many groups chosen

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.260
from five objects have an odd number of members.
